# Not a fixture: a program this slice is required to refuse.
#
# A string interpolation is a shape the emitter does not carry. Its node
# reached the `else` arm of the switch in `emit`, which returned a
# literal zero. The same defect as the unresolved call, one level up.
#
# The interpolation is built and never read, which is the point. An
# array literal would do as well for showing the defect, but not for
# gating it: `xs.size` is a call on what the zero produced, so the call
# refusal catches it whether the shape refusal exists or not, and a
# fixture that passes either way proves nothing. Nothing here asks the
# zero a question. With the shape refusal removed this program emits,
# assembles, runs, and exits 5, which is the right answer to a program
# that is not the one that was written.
#
# `#{node.class}` is the whole message, because the list of shapes that
# reach here is exactly the list a bootstrap still needs.

def answer : Int32
  n = 7
  text = "n is #{n}"
  5
end

__iyi_exit(answer)
